#
# config.table - what each parameter of sd.conf is, for TOOL.CONFIG (carried 10 Nov 2026 from Linux S.75 A6; Windows RELEASE_1.1 218).  One line to a parameter, ten fields
# separated by a vertical bar:
#
#   key | kind | default | min | max | effect | status | text | write | why
#
#   key      the name as sd.conf spells it (gplsrc/config.c read_config() is the list; test-toolconfig-units.py holds this table to it)
#   kind     int, bits (flag bits added together), flag (0 or 1), text, path, float, pair (base,range) or triple (port,user,range)
#   default  what SD uses when sd.conf does not say (the value config.c sets before it reads the file); the shipped sd.conf says more for a few
#   min,max  the range config.c refuses outside of (rangecheck), blank when there is none
#   effect   session: a session reads sd.conf itself when it starts, so an edit reaches the sessions started after it; restart: it sizes or starts the system, so it takes
#            effect when SD is next started; now: a program reads sd.conf each time it uses the parameter, so a change needs no restart and no new session
#   status   live (something acts on it), file (read from sd.conf by a program each time it is used, not by the kernel), inert (accepted, and nothing on Windows acts on
#            it; none since 12 Nov 26, when SPOOLER became the default printer's name, RELEASE_1.1 219)
#   text     one sentence, plain, no vertical bar
#   write    yes: the page and the verb set.config may change it, through SDSYS's elevated helper (gplbld/sd-confset.ps1); no: they may not, and the page shows the line to add
#            by hand.  ONLY A PARAMETER THAT CONFIG.C RANGE-CHECKS CAN BE yes, and the range above is then the range the helper enforces (test-confset-units.ps1 holds the
#            helper and this column to one another).  A door, a path or a command, a log or a security setting, a size config.c does not check, a parameter whose meaning
#            depends on others, one that changes what existing programs compute, and one nothing acts on are all no.
#   why      for a no, one sentence of at most 100 characters, plain, no vertical bar: why this page does not change it; blank for a yes
#
# A line beginning with # is a comment.  Alphabetical by key.  Windows differs from Linux's table in: APIPORT and NETDIRS (Windows parameters), no STARTUP (refused by
# name on Windows), the Windows paths and defaults (the PowerShell shell commands, TEMPDIR from TMP), and SPOOLER a printer name (Linux: a command).
#
APIPORT|int|0|||restart|live|Any value above 0 switches on the API listener, which then listens on the fixed port 4247; 0 or no line leaves it off. The shipped file says 4247.|no|A door: it opens the API listener to the network.
BACKUPDIR|path||||now|file|The directory set.backup.directory saves. The kernel ignores it; the backup and restore programs read sd.conf each time they run, so a change needs no restart.|no|A directory: set.backup.directory saves it, with its own checks of the path.
CLEANUP|int|300|30|3600|restart|live|Seconds between the checks that look for sessions that ended without signing off (a killed terminal, a lost connection) and clear them from the user table.|yes|
CMDSTACK|int|99|||restart|live|How many commands each session keeps on its command stack.|no|Sizes a table in shared memory and SD checks no range: a mistake shows only at the next start.
DEADLOCK|int|0|0|2|restart|live|0 does nothing special; 1 makes SD trap a deadlock between sessions; 2 does that and also writes a file deadlock.n with the locks involved.|no|A diagnostic: 2 makes SD write a file of the locks involved; set it by hand while you hunt one.
DEBUG|bits|0|||restart|live|Flag bits that turn on debugging features of the kernel. Leave at 0 unless asked to change it.|no|Debugging flags of the kernel: they change what SD writes and exposes.
DHCACHE|int|10|0|1024|session|live|How many closed dynamic files SD keeps open for the next use, so that opening them again is quick.|yes|
DUMPDIR|path||||session|live|The directory where a process dump is written. The shipped file names the dumps directory of the system account.|no|A directory: process dumps are written there and can hold what a session had in memory.
ERRLOG|int|0|||restart|live|The size of the error log in kilobytes; a value below 10 is raised to 10 and the oldest entries are discarded when it is full. 0 keeps no limit.|no|Sizes the error log; SD checks no range, and a small size discards its oldest entries sooner.
FDS|int|32767|||restart|live|A limit on the file descriptors SD uses; when it is not set there is no limit of SD's own.|no|Limits the file descriptors SD uses; SD checks no range: a mistake shows at the next start.
FLTDIFF|float|0.0000000000291|||session|live|Two floating point numbers closer together than this compare equal.|no|Changes how every program compares floating point numbers.
FSYNC|bits|0|||session|live|Flag bits that say when SD forces data to disk with fsync: a higher value is safer and slower.|no|Trades the safety of data against speed; flag bits that SD does not check.
GRPDIR|path|C:\ProgramData\SD\group_accounts|||session|live|The directory create.account puts a group account in.|no|A directory: group accounts are made there.
GRPSIZE|int|4|1|8|session|live|The group size, in kilobytes, of a hashed file made without one. 4 since 12 November 2026; an installed system keeps the value its sd.conf has until you change it.|yes|
INTPREC|int|11|0|14|session|live|How many digits of precision integer arithmetic and the INT function keep.|no|Changes the results of arithmetic in programs that already exist.
LGNWAIT|int|0|0|32767|restart|live|Seconds a sign-on waits for a free user number when the user limit is reached; 0 refuses at once.|yes|
LPTRHIGH|int|66|10|32767|session|live|The default page depth of the printer, in lines.|yes|
LPTRWIDE|int|80|10|1000|session|live|The default page width of the printer, in characters.|yes|
MAXCALL|int|10000|10|1000000|session|live|The deepest nesting of subroutine calls one program may reach.|yes|
MAXIDLEN|int|63|63|255|restart|live|The longest record id. 63 is also the lowest value allowed.|no|Structural: the longest record id of the whole system, which reaches every file and program.
MAXRLOCK|int|0|0|100000|session|live|How many record locks one process may hold; 0 sets no limit.|yes|
MUSTLOCK|flag|0|||session|live|1 makes a write or delete fail unless the record is locked first.|no|Makes a write fail unless the record is locked first, which can stop programs that exist.
NETDIRS|text||||restart|live|Directories outside its own account, separated by semicolons, that a session which arrived over the API may reach; empty confines it to its account.|no|A door: it widens what an API session may reach beyond its own account.
NETFILES|bits|0|||restart|live|Bit 2 gates one old API request (number 29); SDNet does not read it. Leave it at 0.|no|A door: bit 2 gates API request 29, a remote open of an account.
NETREPLY|int|30|1|3600|session|live|Seconds to wait for each reply from another SD server once connected; a slower server is treated as lost.|yes|
NETWAIT|int|5|1|3600|session|live|Seconds to wait for a connection to another SD server, for the TCP connect and again for the TLS handshake. 0 is refused.|yes|
NUMFILES|int|80|||restart|live|How many files may be open across all sessions.|no|Sizes the open file table in shared memory; SD checks no range.
NUMLOCKS|int|100|1|32767|restart|live|How many record locks may be held across all sessions.|yes|
NUMUSERS|int|1|||restart|live|How many sessions may run at once; it sizes the user table in shared memory. The shipped file says 20.|no|Sizes the user table, which bounds who can sign on; SD checks no range.
OBJECTS|int|0|||session|live|How many compiled programs one session keeps in memory at once; 0 sets no limit.|no|Limits the programs a session keeps in memory; SD checks no range.
OBJMEM|int|0|||session|live|How much memory, in kilobytes, those programs may take; 0 sets no limit.|no|Limits the memory those programs may take; SD checks no range.
PDUMP|bits|0|||restart|live|Flag bits that say when a process dump is written.|no|Flag bits that say when a process dump is written; a dump can hold what a session had in memory.
PHANTOMS|pair||||restart|live|base,range: user numbers kept for phantoms started with phantom user n. Nobody else is given one.|no|Must agree with USERPOOL: a wrong combination stops SD from starting.
PWDELAY|int|3|0|3600|session|live|Seconds a failed network sign-on is made to wait before the refusal is sent.|no|A security throttle: the pause after a failed network sign-on; 0 removes it.
RECCACHE|int|0|0|32|session|live|How many records each file keeps cached for the next read.|yes|
SAFEDIR|flag|0|||session|live|1 updates directory files by writing a new file and renaming it, rather than in place.|no|Changes how SD replaces the files of a directory file, by writing a new one and renaming it.
SCREENTHEME|text||||session|live|The colour theme a program using the SCREEN statement gets when it does not choose one: paper, night, mono, classic or blue. Blank means mono; the shipped sd.conf sets paper, and a terminal with no colour shows mono whatever this says.|no|Text that SD acts on; SD checks no value.
SDCLIENT|bits|0|||session|live|Restricts what an API session may do. Any non-zero value stops files being opened, and 2 also refuses subroutines not compiled as callable.|no|A door: restricts what an API session may do, and a lower value loosens it.
SDSYS|path||||restart|live|The system account's directory. SD does not start if the global catalogue is not found beneath it. Not readable with the CONFIG function.|no|The system directory: SD cannot start, or finds another system, if it is wrong.
SH|text||||session|live|The command a bare sh starts as an interactive shell; when it is not set SD uses PowerShell with -NoProfile -NoLogo.|no|A command SD runs as an interactive shell.
SH1|text||||session|live|The command sh followed by a command line uses; when it is not set SD uses PowerShell with -NoProfile -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command.|no|A command SD runs for a shell command line.
SORTMEM|int|4096|||session|live|Kilobytes of data above which a sort works on disk instead of in memory.|no|A size in kilobytes that SD does not check, so a mistake shows only when a sort runs.
SORTMRG|int|4|2|10|session|live|How many work files a disk sort merges at once.|yes|
SORTWORK|path||||session|live|The directory for the work files of a sort that does not fit in memory; when it is not set, or does not exist, SD uses TEMPDIR.|no|A directory: the work files of a large sort are written there.
SPOOLER|text||||session|live|The Windows printer a print job goes to when SETPTR names none (Linux: the spooler command). Empty: the Windows default printer.|no|Text that names a printer; check the printer's exact name in Windows first.
TEMPDIR|path||||session|live|The directory for temporary files; when it is not set, or does not exist, SD uses the TMP environment variable.|no|A directory: temporary files are written there.
TERMINFO|path||||session|live|An extra directory of terminfo definitions. The shipped definitions are found without it.|no|A directory: terminal definitions are read from there.
USERPOOL|int|1023|0|16383|restart|live|The highest user number handed out; user numbers rotate through 1 to this value.|no|The highest user number: PHANTOMS is checked against it; a mistake stops SD.
USPLIT|flag|0|0|1|restart|live|1 gives interactive sessions the lowest free user numbers and every other process (a phantom, an API session) the highest.|yes|
USRDIR|path|C:\ProgramData\SD\user_accounts|||session|live|The directory create.account puts a user account in.|no|A directory: user accounts are made there.
YEARBASE|int|1930|||session|live|The base year of a two-digit year: with 1930 the two-digit years run from 1930 to 2029.|no|Changes how every program reads a two-digit year; SD checks no range.
